LOGBOOK.md-17-- STATUS: pending — two board-owner decisions gate CocoaSkills start: approve rc.5 supersession with the 7 enumerated retargets, and require rc.5 build-driver golden regeneration before `TASK-260720-12r55p`.
LOGBOOK.md-18-
LOGBOOK.md-19-### 0440 — Current official Go is newer than the accepted go-v1 family, and ambient GOROOT can misidentify explicit launchers
LOGBOOK.md-20-
LOGBOOK.md:21:- FINDING: the official 2026-07-29 catalog exposes supported Go 1.26.5 and 1.25.12, but accepted Curator `internal/godriver/session.go` allowlists only family `1.25`. The correct bootstrap for current `go-v1` qualification is Go 1.25.12; Go 1.26 must fail closed until the family passes the same conformance vectors and the allowlist is deliberately expanded.
LOGBOOK.md-22-- FINDING: local PATH resolves a 411-byte goenv shell shim before two native launchers. Three real arm64 trees exist — goenv 1.25.5, Homebrew 1.25.5, official `/usr/local/go` package 1.25.1 — but none is current or automatically trusted. The shell exports goenv `GOROOT`; without clearing it, direct Homebrew and `/usr/local/go/bin/go` calls both reported the goenv root. Empty-environment `GOENV=off GOTOOLCHAIN=local` probes restored the correct roots.
LOGBOOK.md:23:- FINDING: read-only `ssh win` inventory identifies Windows 10 Pro x64 but no `go.exe` on process/user/machine PATH, no Go MSI/uninstall registry entry, and no tree at conventional MSI/archive/package-manager roots. Windows qualification needs a human-installed, manager-approved Go 1.25.12 amd64 root; PATH search or automatic installation is not a product remedy.
LOGBOOK.md:24:- FINDING: late read-only `ssh lev` inventory identifies Ubuntu 26.04 LTS x86_64 with no Go on PATH, no conventional Go root or installed `golang*` package, and root-owned `0755` parents under `/usr/local` and `/opt`. Ubuntu offers Go 1.26, which is outside the accepted family; later Linux qualification needs a manually installed/approved official Go 1.25.12 linux-amd64 tree.
LOGBOOK.md-25-- DECISION INPUT: official archive SHA-256 authenticates the downloaded artifact but does not replace `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, which fingerprints the admitted full tree plus normalized `go version`. Catalog guidance must keep URLs/checksums/absolute roots manager-owned, render commands without executing them, set `GOTOOLCHAIN=local`, and reject missing, ambient, package-selected, wrapper, or unknown-family toolchains.
LOGBOOK.md-26-- EVIDENCE: `.research/260729_official-go-bootstrap-guidance.md`; official `go.dev/dl`, install, manage-install, toolchain, and release-policy sources retrieved 2026-07-29. Local and corrected encoded Windows SSH inventories exited 0; the first Windows attempt exited 1 on PowerShell quoting. The first Linux inventory exited 127 after an unescaped read-only `dpkg-query` format tripped `set -u`; its corrected package/root/permission probe exited 0. Neither failed attempt mutated a host or supplied an accepted conclusion.
LOGBOOK.md-27-
LOGBOOK.md-28-### 0432 — Swift cycle-2 review: green boundary evidence still left toolchain identity open
--
LOGBOOK.md-941-- FINDING: probes now perform the *exact* operation of the operation — the real Job Object with this build's `ActiveProcessLimit`/memory limits, and a real `setrlimit` to this build's byte bound followed by a restore — rather than a generic capability check.
LOGBOOK.md-942-- FIX (R2): `runGo` dereferenced `command.ProcessState` on any `Run` error, but it is nil when process creation fails. Now nil-safe: a refusal sets `StartFailed`, `ExitCode = -1`, and the parent classifies it as the existing `go_list_failed`/`go_build_failed`. `Started` is documented as "times the single permitted creation site was used", so a refused attempt still consumes one and the exact-count process-graph guard is unchanged. A launcher identity failure now ends the session with its own stable code instead of a result with `Started == 0` that the parent misreported as a graph violation.
LOGBOOK.md-943-- FINDING: a real, portable injection for the nil-`ProcessState` path is a manager-owned working directory that is inside the frozen snapshot but does not exist — the OS refuses `chdir` on both macOS and Windows and leaves no process state. On Windows the Job Object `ActiveProcessLimit = 1` additionally drives the same refusal through a complete `Build`, which is the closest thing to the scenario the review described.
LOGBOOK.md-944-- ANOMALY: `cmd … & echo %ERRORLEVEL%` over ssh expands at parse time and always printed `0`. Every Windows gate reading taken that way was discarded and re-run with runtime `&& echo EXIT_0 || echo EXIT_NONZERO`. Related: `set VAR=1 && …` in `cmd.exe` keeps the trailing space, so `CURATOR_REAL_GO_BUILD_TEST` was `"1 "` and the real-build test silently skipped; the quoted `set "VAR=1"` form fixes it. Both are traps for any future native Windows evidence run.
LOGBOOK.md:945:- EVIDENCE: local macOS arm64 build/vet/gofmt/full/`-race`/rc.5/rc.4/real-build/scoped-lint all exit 0. macOS amd64 (`ssh relux`) and Windows amd64 (`ssh win`) verified byte-identical (143 `cmd`+`internal` Go files, per-file SHA-256 diff clean) with build, vet, task packages under the rc.5 root, and the real vendored build all exit 0. Windows `go test ./...` still exits non-zero in the same five pre-existing packages outside this task.
LOGBOOK.md-946-- STATUS: handed to review.
LOGBOOK.md-947-
LOGBOOK.md-948-### 0740 — Curator implements portable manager-worker-v1 go-v1
LOGBOOK.md-949-- MILESTONE: `internal/godriver` now runs every source-aware Go command inside one identity-verified hidden-mode re-execution of the manager (`cmd/curator` `__curator-go-worker-v1`), with a nonce-bound list → parent graph validation → HMAC build permit → build state machine. The declarative `HostPolicy` adapter the earlier review rejected is deleted; its environment/argv/root checks now live in the worker where they govern a real process.
